---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import Term from '../../components/journey/Term.astro';
import LessonRef from '../../components/journey/LessonRef.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';

const ids = [
  't3code.ownLogins', 't3code.install', 't3code.remote', 't3code.connectMachines', 't3code.phoneIsRemote', 't3code.delegate',
  'harnesses.claudeCode.web', 'harnesses.codex.sandbox', 'plans.chatgpt.limits.cloudTasks',
  'harnesses.claudeCode.bestPractice', 'harnesses.claudeCode.permissionModes', 't3code.permissionModes', 't3code.defaultMode',
];
---
<Journey chapter="where" first="laptop" title="Other machines" description="Where an agent runs: on your laptop, on an always-on computer, or on a machine in the cloud.">
  <Step id="laptop" title="So far it all ran on your laptop">
    <p>Start here: one job on your laptop, with T3 Code installed and the agent signed in with your own account. Every job in <LessonRef id="jobs" /> ran like this, on your files and your logins.</p>
    <ForLevel is="engineer"><p>On the laptop everything lives in one place: the repository, the threads, the logins and the app’s secrets in a local <code>.env</code> file.</p></ForLevel>
    <More title="In T3 Code">
      <p><Fact id="t3code.install" show="claim" /></p>
      <p><Fact id="t3code.ownLogins" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="lid" title="Close the lid and it stops">
    <p>The laptop sleeps and its job stops where it was. Open the lid and it carries on.</p>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="The laptop’s lid">
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="open" data-default aria-pressed="true">Lid open</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="closed" aria-pressed="false">Lid closed</button>
    </div>
  </Step>
  <Step id="box" title="An always-on computer">
    <p>A desktop or a small home server that never sleeps keeps working with the lid closed, and you follow it from your phone. New T3 Code threads start in <Fact id="t3code.defaultMode" />, which edits and runs commands without asking, so pick the <Term id="permission-mode" /> on purpose before you walk away.</p>
    <ForLevel is="engineer"><p>The repository, the threads and the agent’s logins live on that computer; laptop and phone only show them. The app’s secrets go in that machine’s environment, never in the repository.</p></ForLevel>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="The laptop’s lid">
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="open" aria-pressed="false">Lid open</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="closed" data-default aria-pressed="true">Lid closed</button>
    </div>
    <More title="How, in T3 Code">
      <ol>
        <li>Install T3 Code on the always-on computer too.</li>
        <li>Reach it from the laptop through one of: <Fact id="t3code.remote" />. Tailscale is a private network between your own devices; an SSH environment is a terminal login to that computer; T3 Connect is T3 Code’s own account that links your machines.</li>
        <li>A free T3 Connect account links up to <Fact id="t3code.connectMachines" /> machines.</li>
        <li>Follow it from your phone. <Fact id="t3code.phoneIsRemote" show="claim" /></li>
        <li>From a thread on the laptop, an agent can hand that computer a task with <Fact id="t3code.delegate" />.</li>
        <li>The modes: <Fact id="t3code.permissionModes" show="claim" /><ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']} inline> In Claude Code alone the names differ: <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.permissionModes" show="claim" /></ForLevel></li>
      </ol>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="cloud" title="A machine in the cloud">
    <p>It starts empty for each task, with the repository and only what you set up, and the result comes back to the repository as a branch. Without the test database the tests need, the check cannot run.</p>
    <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']}><p>Give it a setup script that installs dependencies and starts the test database, so <code>npm test</code> runs there as it does on your laptop.</p></ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer"><p>Nothing survives between tasks: state is the repository, the setup script and the secrets you store in the service. <Fact id="plans.chatgpt.limits.cloudTasks" show="claim" /></p></ForLevel>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="What the cloud machine is given">
      <button type="button" data-do="setup" data-val="without" data-default aria-pressed="true">Without the setup</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="setup" data-val="with" aria-pressed="false">With it</button>
    </div>
    <More title="How, in the harnesses">
      <ol>
        <li><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.web" show="claim" /></li>
        <li><Fact id="harnesses.codex.sandbox" show="claim" /></li>
        <li><Fact id="plans.chatgpt.limits.cloudTasks" show="claim" /></li>
      </ol>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="mix" title="Which job goes where">
    <p>Live work on the laptop, long runs on the always-on computer, separate tasks in the cloud, all landing on one main. Halfway through the hour the lid closes; watch which jobs keep landing.</p>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="The laptop’s lid">
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="open" data-default aria-pressed="true">Lid open</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="lid" data-val="closed" aria-pressed="false">Lid closed</button>
    </div>
    <More title="Before you walk away">
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.bestPractice" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
    <p class="j-end">This is the last lesson. Next you leave the lessons and build something: <a href={url('start/first-hour/')}>the first hour</a>, starting on your laptop.</p>
    <Next from="where" more={[{ href: 'wiki/always-on-machine/', text: 'An always-on computer', side: true }, { href: 'wiki/secrets/', text: 'Secrets a machine needs', side: true }, { href: 'wiki/permission-mode/', text: 'Permission modes', side: true }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The jobs, the hour and the missing test database are made up to show the mechanism.</p>
    <Verified ids={ids} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
